variants.reconstruction:
%n [sippu | arattû || linaššiq]ū | ka[ppīka]
#note: See @bib{RN117@336}: “i 1'. As restored this line differs only from SB VI 15 in the choice of part of the body, @i{kappīka} for @i{idīka}.”
// L I.4 SB "VI" 15

%n [lū kamsū | ina šaplīka || šarrū (|) be]lū | ellûni[kku]
#note: See @bib{RN117@336}: “i 2'. Though a precative is expected and @i{lu-ú} suggests @i{lū}, I cannot find a precative @i{lū el-lu-ni-}[(…)] that gives satisfactory meaning. Accordingly I follow Arnaud in assuming that this line agrees closely with SB VI 16. The restoration of @i{be}]@i{-lu-ú} is suggested by the SB variant @i{šarrū bēlū u rubû} (MS Q). The verb @i{ellûni}[@i{kku}] stands in place of the SB noun @i{rubû}.”
// L I.4 SB "VI" 16

%n [... (|) liqit | šadî (|) u māti || li]ššûnikku | bi[lta]
#note: See @bib{RN117@336}: “i 3'. For @i{liššûnikku} SB VI 17 has the precative stative @i{lū našûnikka}.”
// L I.4 SB "VI" 17

%n [enzātūka | takšê | lahrātūka | tūʾa]mī? | lūlidā
#note: See @bib{RN117@336}: “i 4'. The word before @i{lūlidā} was restored by Arnaud as @akk{tu-ʾ-a-m]a}, ‘a twin’, though he translated it as plural (‘des jumeaux’). However, this reading is uncertain for two reasons: (a) the broken sign is not a convincing @i{ma}, and (b) @i{tuʾāma} in the singular would yield very odd sense, for a dual is required. All three manuscripts of SB VI 18 do indeed have good dual forms (@i{tuʾāmī} and @i{tuʾāmē}). Perhaps read @akk{MAŠ.TAB.B]A?} Otherwise I can offer no solution to this problem.”
// L I.4 SB "VI" 18

%n [... | lu]mši? | bubūtī-ma | ku[rummatī?]
#note: See @bib{RN117@336}: “i 9'. The order of the two nouns is transposed in SB VI 26: @i{kurummatī u bubūtī}. The apparent use in the present line of enclitic @i{-ma} for the conjunction is poor Akkadian.”
// L I.4 SB "VI" 26

%n [...] ... | simdi ... [...]
#note: See @bib{RN117@336–337}: “i 10'. The phrase preserved in this line was read @akk{si-in-ti₄ qa-t[i} by Arnaud (‘ornament de la ma[in’); @i{sinti} or @i{sindi} is MB for @i{simti}. Collation does not favour @i{qa} (compare the examples in ii 6'–7') but did produce any clear alternative (the trace of the sign following it is now missing entirely). The counterpart of this line in the SB text is evidently the couplet VI 27–8: [@i{tušakkalanni}? @i{a}]@i{kla simat ilūti} / [@i{kurunna tašaqq}]@i{â} @i{simat šarrūti}. Just possibly one should emend MB Emar2 to a ‘Sandhi’-type writing, @akk{si-in-di-lu₄!-t[i]}.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "VI" 27

%n [... | halp]û? | lā kāṣirat | [šurīpi]
#note: See @bib{RN117@337}: “i 13'. If the restoration is correct, @i{ḫalpû} is feminine. Winter frosts are less common in the Mesopotamian plain than in the surrounding uplands but the image of congealed ice on a frozen pond and other standing water would not be alien to a Babylonian poet.”
// L I.4 SB "VI" 33

%n [dalat (|) arkabinni | š]a lā ikallû || [š]ā[ra | u zīqa]
#note: See @bib{RN117@337}: “i 14'. For the @i{dalat arkabinni} see Chapter 13, the commentary on SB VI 34.”
// L I.4 SB "VI" 34

%n nādu? | muna[kkisat? | nāšîša]
#note: See @bib{RN117@337}: “i 16'. If it were not for the determinative @sux{kuš}, it would not be certain that this line is the counterpart of SB VI 38. The exact spelling of @i{nādu} here is not clear to me. The late text has a different participle, probably @i{murassât}.”
// L I.4 SB "VI" 38

%n [y]ašubû | muʾabbitu (||) dūr (|) abni
#note: See @bib{RN117@337}: “i 17'. On the relationship of this line to SB VI 39–40 see the commentary ad loc. As a construct state @i{muʾabbitu} exhibits the literary final @i{-u}.”
// L I.4 SB "VI" 40

%n [šēnu | munaššikat || šēp] (|) mubeʾʾi (|) sūqi
#note: See @bib{RN117@337}: “i 18'. SB VI 41 has simply @i{šēpī bēlīšu}. For ‘biting’ shoes see the commentary thereon. The MB form @i{mubeʾʾi} attests to the very rare intensive II/1 stem of @i{bâʾu}, ignored by the dictionaries. Another unambiguous II/1 form (with ventive @i{-u}) occurs in the Babylonian Theodicy 291: @i{ri-bit āli}(uru)@i{-ia ú-ba-ʾ-ú ni-ḫi-iš}, ‘I would pass through the square of my city quietly’ (@bib{RN432@88}, var. I/1 @i{a-ba-ʾ}).”
// L I.4 SB "VI" 41

%n [ayyû | hāʾerki || ilb]er | dāriš
#note: See @bib{RN117@337}: “i 19'. In the late text the counterpart of the verb @i{ilber} is @i{ibūr}.”
// L I.4 SB "VI" 42

%n [ana dumuzi | hāʾir (|) ṣu]hrēt[īk]i!
#note: See @bib{RN117@337}: “i 22'. The masculine suffix for the feminine is a gross solecism, even at Emar.”
// L I.4 SB "VI" 46

%n [tar]ʾamī-ma | [kī? ina sup]ūri | ašbātī-ma
#note: See @bib{RN117@338}: “i 32'–4'. These three lines are somewhat different from the SB text, which reads (SB VI 58–60): @i{tarāmīma rēʾâ nāqida utulla} / [@i{ša k}]@i{ayyānamma tumrī išpukakki} / [@i{ūm}]@i{išamma uṭabbaḫakki unīqēti}. First, both the third-person verbs are plural, which supposes a misunderstanding of the nouns in apposition as different individuals; second, Ištar’s sojourn in the sheepfold receives specific mention. Her visit there is also evoked in a MB ballad (@bib{black1983babylonian@31, 36}): @i{ta-ku-uš iš-tar a-na qé-reb su-pu-ri-šu-ma}, ‘Ištar walked right into his sheepfold’.”
// L I.4 SB "VI" 58

%n [rēʾâ | (u) utulla | ša k]ayyānum-ma | ištēn (|) immera | idūkūnikki
// L I.4 SB "VI" 58
// L I.4 SB "VI" 59

%n abu | [lā attī | tegrî || šarra] | gilgāmeš
#note: See @bib{RN117@338}: “ii 2'. MB Emar has @i{a-bu} with SB MS a₂ (Aššur) against the Kuyunjik sources’ @i{a-ba}.”
// L I.4 SB "VI" 89

%n u [gilgāmeš | errēt]īki | pīš[ē]tīki | im[ennu]
#note: See @bib{RN117@338}: “ii 3'–4'. The line is slightly reworked in SB VI 90, which exists in two versions: @i{u Gilgāmeš umannâ pīšātīki / pīšātīki u errētīki} (MSS Oa) and @i{Gilgāmeš umannâ pīšātīki} [@i{u errētīki}] (MS Q).”
// cf. L I.4 SB "VI" 90
// cf. L I.4 SB "VI" 91

%n iz[zaqqara | ana ānim | abīša]
#note: See @bib{RN117@338}: “ii 2". This spelling of @i{issaqqara} appears in the Emar manuscript of the fable of Tamarisk and Date-Palm, which uses a variant narrative formula for introducing direct speech: @i{ut-te-er pâ}(@akk{|KA×U|})@i{-ša i-ta-pa-al iz-za-qa-ra a-na} etc.: see @bib{RN649@177, 60′}.”
// L I.4 SB "VI" 107

%n ānu(m) | [išm]e | qabâša
#note: See @bib{RN117@338}: “ii 6′′. A fuller version of this short line appears in the SB text as @i{išmēma Anu annâ qabâ Ištar}.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "VI" 113

%n urr[ad | ana nāri | sebe | ammat | nāru] | imtaṭṭi
#note: See @bib{RN117@338}: “c ii 9′′–10′′. Restored after the late text, SB VI 118: @i{ūrid ana nārimma sebe} (@i{ina}) @i{ammat nāru umdaṭṭi}.”
// L I.4 SB "VI" 118
